claims.paper_wording:
Contribution 3: predicting tipping. On identical snapshots we compare several monitors:
- a clock that sees only the round number;
- gradient-boosted classifiers on aggregated communication features, with graph-level features and with per-agent memory;
- an edge-level monitor;
- a temporal graph neural network with per-agent memory, alone and blended with the gradient-boosted model;
- zero-shot LLM monitors (Claude Haiku 5.5) that read either the whole transcript or one conversation at a time.
In cross-validation on the 64 training configurations, every communication monitor beats the clock at matched time (Holm-adjusted p ≤ 0.0011). The blend and the per-conversation LLM monitor also beat the aggregated-feature model (+0.048 and +0.068 AUROC). On the 36 held-out configurations, all monitors reach 0.80 to 0.86 AUROC, and none of the 11 pre-specified comparisons survives Holm correction.

reviews.corrected_paper_wording:
Contribution 3: predicting tipping. On identical snapshots we compare several monitors:
- a clock that sees only the round number;
- gradient-boosted classifiers on aggregated communication features, with graph-level features and with per-agent memory;
- an edge-level monitor;
- a temporal GNN with per-agent memory, alone and blended with gradient boosting;
- zero-shot Claude Haiku 5.5 monitors that read either the whole transcript or one conversation at a time.
Cross-validation on the 64 training configurations is descriptive in our analysis plan, and the GNN was selected on the same rows. There, the rungs order as expected (AUROC: clock 0.75, pairwise edge features 0.79, aggregated features 0.82, plus graph features 0.84, GNN 0.88). All five primary communication monitors beat the clock at matched time (Holm-adjusted p = 0.0011 each). The blend and the per-conversation LLM monitor beat aggregated features (+0.048 and +0.068 AUROC). These advantages do not replicate on the 36 held-out configurations. There, the communication monitors reach 0.80-0.86 AUROC. The GNN ties with gradient boosting on aggregated and graph features (0.817 vs 0.812, Holm p = 1.0). The blend and the LLM monitor lie +0.020 and +0.042 above it (Holm p = 0.78), and none of the 11 pre-specified comparisons survives Holm correction. In the validation, the pre-registered ordering pairwise < aggregated <= aggregated + graph <= GNN failed in all four arms.

We did not vary the prediction horizon systematically, so we show no horizon curve. We ran an exploratory sensitivity analysis on out-of-fold predictions pooled over all 112 night configurations, test configurations included. There, AUROC for Delta = 2, 3 and 5 rounds changes by at most 0.04:
- per-conversation LLM monitor (prompt fixed at Delta = 3): 0.903 / 0.890 / 0.866;
- gradient boosting on aggregated features (refit for each Delta): 0.850 / 0.864 / 0.845;
- clock: 0.780 / 0.783 / 0.780.
The frozen GNN, trained for Delta = 3, reaches 0.809 on the held-out splits when scored against Delta = 5 (0.817 for Delta = 3).
